/// Helper: dedicated HR measure container holding all 120 explicit analytical and context measures in organized display folders. Its single hidden Anchor row has no business fact grain or analytical key; it intentionally has no relationship. Measures retrieve populations from the conformed dimensions and domain facts.
table Measures_HR
	lineageTag: ab80d181-a8e4-48e4-8359-96835406041d

	/// Latest recorded payroll month end used as the reporting-cutoff proxy. Calculation: Global maximum Payroll Period, advanced to its month end; REMOVEFILTERS clears all report filters. Purpose: A common endpoint prevents lifecycle views from drifting beyond the latest payroll month. Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy. Date/filter context: Global extract context; REMOVEFILTERS ignores Date, Department and all other report filters. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Not today, a refresh timestamp, or an independent completeness check; attendance may stop earlier. Interpretation: Read as the chosen cutoff proxy, not proof that every HR domain or the payroll month is complete.
	measure 'Data Through Date' =
			// Latest recorded payroll month end used as the reporting-cutoff proxy.
			// Why: A common endpoint prevents lifecycle views from drifting beyond the latest payroll month.
			// Logic: Global maximum Payroll Period, advanced to its month end; REMOVEFILTERS clears all report filters.
			// Context: Global extract context; REMOVEFILTERS ignores Date, Department and all other report filters.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy.
			// Caveat: Not today, a refresh timestamp, or an independent completeness check; attendance may stop earlier.
			EOMONTH(CALCULATE(MAX('Fact_Payroll'[Payroll Period]), REMOVEFILTERS()),0)
		formatString: dd MMM yyyy
		displayFolder: 00 Context
		lineageTag: 5d877080-7200-4136-a374-f77a383eaf82

	/// First calendar date remaining in the selected date context. Calculation: Minimum Dim_Date[Date]. Purpose: A single start date defines the lifecycle and equivalent-period windows. Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy. Date/filter context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Uses the selected minimum; disjoint selections are treated as one min/max interval by lifecycle measures. Interpretation: Use with Period End Date to read the effective analysis interval.
	measure 'Period Start Date' =
			// First calendar date remaining in the selected date context.
			// Why: A single start date defines the lifecycle and equivalent-period windows.
			// Logic: Minimum Dim_Date[Date].
			// Context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy.
			// Caveat: Uses the selected minimum; disjoint selections are treated as one min/max interval by lifecycle measures.
			MIN('Dim_Date'[Date])
		formatString: dd MMM yyyy
		displayFolder: 00 Context
		lineageTag: 7a17ade2-87b5-4fa3-b37e-9ac64c3f8a3a

	/// Last selected calendar date, capped at payroll coverage. Calculation: The smaller of maximum selected Dim_Date[Date] and Data Through Date. Purpose: Prevents a partial source year from being presented as a complete lifecycle year. Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy. Date/filter context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Base transaction totals follow their own date roles. With an empty Date context, MIN can coerce BLANK to date-origin zero; no new empty-context guard is added. Interpretation: The effective snapshot endpoint can be earlier than the selected calendar end.
	measure 'Period End Date' =
			// Last selected calendar date, capped at payroll coverage.
			// Why: Prevents a partial source year from being presented as a complete lifecycle year.
			// Logic: The smaller of maximum selected Dim_Date[Date] and Data Through Date.
			// Context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Calendar date, displayed dd MMM yyyy.
			// Caveat: Base transaction totals follow their own date roles. With an empty Date context, MIN can coerce BLANK to date-origin zero; no new empty-context guard is added.
			MIN(MAX('Dim_Date'[Date]),[Data Through Date])
		formatString: dd MMM yyyy
		displayFolder: 00 Context
		lineageTag: 4306ea4c-0c6e-4af1-8cae-790c78ff528b

	/// Readable effective date-window caption. Calculation: Formats Period Start Date and Period End Date as dd MMM yyyy and joins them using the stored separator. Purpose: Makes the capped analysis window visible to report users. Unit: Text. Date/filter context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Text only; it does not apply a filter, validate an empty Date context, or suppress an inverted future-only caption. Stored separator is preserved exactly. Interpretation: Read this alongside date slicers before comparing periods.
	measure 'Selected Period Label' =
			// Readable effective date-window caption.
			// Why: Makes the capped analysis window visible to report users.
			// Logic: Formats Period Start Date and Period End Date as dd MMM yyyy and joins them using the stored separator.
			// Context: Current Dim_Date selection; endpoint is capped where referenced. Department and employee selections do not change the global coverage cutoff.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Text.
			// Caveat: Text only; it does not apply a filter, validate an empty Date context, or suppress an inverted future-only caption. Stored separator is preserved exactly.
			FORMAT([Period Start Date],"dd MMM yyyy") & " – " & FORMAT([Period End Date],"dd MMM yyyy")
		formatString: @
		displayFolder: 00 Context
		lineageTag: ae4905dd-95d3-4230-92be-d1638ff8f94d

	/// Employee master records matching current dimension filters, including former employees. Calculation: Count of rows in Dim_Employee. Purpose: Separates the size of the employee register from lifecycle-active workforce. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Department and employee attributes apply. Dim_Date does not directly filter Dim_Employee; all employment dates remain represented. Empty/zero behavior: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Limitations: Calendar has no direct relationship to Employee, so date slicers do not reduce this master count. Interpretation: A higher value means more employee spells recorded, not necessarily more current staff.
	measure 'Total Employees' =
			// Employee master records matching current dimension filters, including former employees.
			// Why: Separates the size of the employee register from lifecycle-active workforce.
			// Logic: Count of rows in Dim_Employee.
			// Context: Department and employee attributes apply. Dim_Date does not directly filter Dim_Employee; all employment dates remain represented.
			// Empty/zero: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Calendar has no direct relationship to Employee, so date slicers do not reduce this master count.
			COUNTROWS('Dim_Employee')
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 93308602-9320-4108-8501-3bac5d95300b

	/// Employees still employed at the capped selected endpoint, including employees on leave. Calculation: Hire Date on or before the endpoint and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the endpoint. Purpose: Reconstructs workforce from employment dates instead of current status labels. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Wholly future windows return BLANK; no matching employees can remain BLANK. Historical department/job transfers are unavailable. Interpretation: Represents available employment population at that date, not FTE or recorded attendance.
	measure 'Active Headcount As Of Date' =
			// Employees still employed at the capped selected endpoint, including employees on leave.
			// Why: Reconstructs workforce from employment dates instead of current status labels.
			// Logic: Hire Date on or before the endpoint and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the endpoint.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Wholly future windows return BLANK; no matching employees can remain BLANK. Historical department/job transfers are unavailable.
			VAR _d=[Period End Date] RETURN IF([Period Start Date]>[Data Through Date],BLANK(),COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<=_d && (ISBLANK('Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]) || 'Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]>_d))))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 1efa4815-20cd-47c6-b7d6-2144a76d4747

	/// Employees employed on the day before the selected interval begins. Calculation: Hire Date on or before the day before Period Start Date and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the day before Period Start Date. Purpose: Provides the opening population for rollforward and retention. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Wholly future windows return BLANK; empty COUNTROWS remains BLANK rather than forced zero. Interpretation: Compare with Closing Headcount and movement within the interval.
	measure 'Opening Headcount' =
			// Employees employed on the day before the selected interval begins.
			// Why: Provides the opening population for rollforward and retention.
			// Logic: Hire Date on or before the day before Period Start Date and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the day before Period Start Date.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Wholly future windows return BLANK; empty COUNTROWS remains BLANK rather than forced zero.
			VAR _d=[Period Start Date]-1 RETURN IF([Period Start Date]>[Data Through Date],BLANK(),COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<=_d && (ISBLANK('Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]) || 'Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]>_d))))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 565655ec-6c8c-48d8-9285-9a40d0e4ca75

	/// Employees employed at the capped selected interval end. Calculation: Hire Date on or before the endpoint and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the endpoint. COALESCE converts an empty valid population to zero. Purpose: Provides an explicit closing balance for workforce rollforward. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Wholly future windows return BLANK; current employee classifications are used for historical segmentation. Interpretation: Opening plus hires minus exits should reconcile to this balance.
	measure 'Closing Headcount' =
			// Employees employed at the capped selected interval end.
			// Why: Provides an explicit closing balance for workforce rollforward.
			// Logic: Hire Date on or before the endpoint and no Exit Date, or an Exit Date strictly after the endpoint. COALESCE converts an empty valid population to zero.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Wholly future windows return BLANK; current employee classifications are used for historical segmentation.
			VAR _d=[Period End Date] RETURN IF([Period Start Date]>[Data Through Date],BLANK(),COALESCE(COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<=_d && (ISBLANK('Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]) || 'Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]>_d))),0))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 5478d9df-9df9-449c-9155-cc13278f58de

	/// Employee master records whose Hire Date falls within the selected min/max interval. Calculation: Count Employee Hire Date between Period Start Date and capped Period End Date, inclusive; empty valid counts become zero. Purpose: Measures employee-master starts independently of candidate application outcomes. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: One supplied hire date per employee; rehire history is unavailable. Future-only windows return BLANK. Recruitment Hired outcomes are a different unlinked population. Interpretation: A positive value indicates recorded employee inflow; zero is a verified no-hire interval in this extract.
	measure 'New Hires' =
			// Employee master records whose Hire Date falls within the selected min/max interval.
			// Why: Measures employee-master starts independently of candidate application outcomes.
			// Logic: Count Employee Hire Date between Period Start Date and capped Period End Date, inclusive; empty valid counts become zero.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: One supplied hire date per employee; rehire history is unavailable. Future-only windows return BLANK. Recruitment Hired outcomes are a different unlinked population.
			VAR _s=[Period Start Date] VAR _e=[Period End Date] RETURN IF(_s>_e,BLANK(),COALESCE(COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]>=_s && 'Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<=_e)),0))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: fa9f8185-8932-4a5d-bc79-b7673a8c554d

	/// Exit events dated in the selected reporting context. Calculation: Count Fact_Exit rows under its active Exit Date relationship; future-only lifecycle windows return BLANK. Purpose: Measures workforce outflow on the actual exit date. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Exit Date actively filters exit rows; Department and employee filters also apply. A future-only selected interval is suppressed using the global payroll cutoff; no payroll-period relationship filters the numerator. Empty/zero behavior: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Empty COUNTROWS can be BLANK. Retirement and other exit types remain included; not only voluntary/involuntary. Interpretation: Compare with hires and opening population before interpreting turnover.
	measure 'Employee Exits' =
			// Exit events dated in the selected reporting context.
			// Why: Measures workforce outflow on the actual exit date.
			// Logic: Count Fact_Exit rows under its active Exit Date relationship; future-only lifecycle windows return BLANK.
			// Context: Exit Date actively filters exit rows; Department and employee filters also apply. A future-only selected interval is suppressed using the global payroll cutoff; no payroll-period relationship filters the numerator.
			// Empty/zero: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Empty COUNTROWS can be BLANK. Retirement and other exit types remain included; not only voluntary/involuntary.
			IF([Period Start Date]>[Data Through Date],BLANK(),COUNTROWS('Fact_Exit'))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 83ac92ad-dab6-4f2f-a44b-cb1e7e1f29b1

	/// Exact mean end-of-day lifecycle workforce across the selected contiguous interval. Calculation: Generate every day between selected start and capped end, count employed employees each day including zero-population days, then average. Purpose: A daily exposure denominator is more defensible than averaging only the two endpoints. Unit: Employees averaged across every day in the interval. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Averages ignore BLANK values; a mean without qualifying observations is BLANK unless zero is explicitly supplied. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Includes on-leave employees; disjoint date selections are treated as their contiguous min/max span. Future-only windows return BLANK. Interpretation: Use as the population denominator for period attrition.
	measure 'Average Daily Headcount' =
			// Exact mean end-of-day lifecycle workforce across the selected contiguous interval.
			// Why: A daily exposure denominator is more defensible than averaging only the two endpoints.
			// Logic: Generate every day between selected start and capped end, count employed employees each day including zero-population days, then average.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COALESCE explicitly substitutes zero only where present in the expression. Averages ignore BLANK values; a mean without qualifying observations is BLANK unless zero is explicitly supplied. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Employees averaged across every day in the interval.
			// Caveat: Includes on-leave employees; disjoint date selections are treated as their contiguous min/max span. Future-only windows return BLANK.
			VAR _s=[Period Start Date] VAR _e=[Period End Date] RETURN IF(_s>_e,BLANK(),AVERAGEX(CALENDAR(_s,_e),VAR _d=[Date] RETURN COALESCE(COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<=_d && (ISBLANK('Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]) || 'Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]>_d))),0)))
		formatString: #,##0.0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: aab4963e-c8ac-4c84-8534-d5e66235be0c

	/// Employee inflow less employee outflow during the selected interval. Calculation: New Hires minus Employee Exits. Purpose: Shows whether hiring offsets departures. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Uses source lifecycle events and inherits their date filters and DAX blank-to-number arithmetic. Interpretation: Positive means workforce growth; negative means contraction, without implying either is desirable.
	measure 'Net Workforce Change' =
			// Employee inflow less employee outflow during the selected interval.
			// Why: Shows whether hiring offsets departures.
			// Logic: New Hires minus Employee Exits.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Uses source lifecycle events and inherits their date filters and DAX blank-to-number arithmetic.
			[New Hires]-[Employee Exits]
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 01 Workforce
		lineageTag: 1407c5cc-b26a-42a5-a6e4-18c8a04c688b

	/// Unexplained difference between observed closing workforce and expected workforce movement. Calculation: Closing Headcount minus Opening Headcount minus New Hires plus Employee Exits. Purpose: A reconciliation control checks that lifecycle dates and period movements agree. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Opening/closing and hires use explicit lifecycle min/max windows; exits follow active Exit Date. Endpoint is capped at global payroll coverage. Department and employee classifications remain extract values. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: Disjoint dates: lifecycle windows span selected min/max while exit rows retain the selected date set. Current classifications may not reflect historical transfers; no transfer history exists. Interpretation: Zero is expected for a consistent contiguous interval; investigate a nonzero result.
	measure 'Headcount Rollforward Residual' =
			// Unexplained difference between observed closing workforce and expected workforce movement.
			// Why: A reconciliation control checks that lifecycle dates and period movements agree.
			// Logic: Closing Headcount minus Opening Headcount minus New Hires plus Employee Exits.
			// Context: Opening/closing and hires use explicit lifecycle min/max windows; exits follow active Exit Date. Endpoint is capped at global payroll coverage. Department and employee classifications remain extract values.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Disjoint dates: lifecycle windows span selected min/max while exit rows retain the selected date set. Current classifications may not reflect historical transfers; no transfer history exists.
			[Closing Headcount]-[Opening Headcount]-[New Hires]+[Employee Exits]
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 09 Validation
		lineageTag: 3f11a512-9000-4965-b1cb-0752beae7aba

	/// Departures during the selected interval relative to average daily workforce exposure. Calculation: Employee Exits / Average Daily Headcount. Purpose: Uses all exit events and a daily lifecycle population rather than total employee-master records. Unit: Ratio stored as a fraction; displayed as a percentage with one decimal place. Date/filter context: Numerator follows active Exit Date; denominator counts lifecycle-active employees on every day of the selected min/max interval, capped at global payroll coverage. Department and employee filters apply to both populations. Empty/zero behavior: DIVIDE returns BLANK for a zero or BLANK denominator; a BLANK numerator also stays BLANK even with a positive denominator. No alternate result is supplied. Limitations: Zero or BLANK denominator returns BLANK. Disjoint dates: daily lifecycle denominator spans selected min/max while exit rows retain selected dates. Prefer contiguous equivalent intervals. Interpretation: Higher means more turnover during the selected period; this is not an annualized rate.
	measure 'Attrition Rate' =
			// Departures during the selected interval relative to average daily workforce exposure.
			// Why: Uses all exit events and a daily lifecycle population rather than total employee-master records.
			// Logic: Employee Exits / Average Daily Headcount.
			// Context: Numerator follows active Exit Date; denominator counts lifecycle-active employees on every day of the selected min/max interval, capped at global payroll coverage. Department and employee filters apply to both populations.
			// Empty/zero: DIVIDE returns BLANK for a zero or BLANK denominator; a BLANK numerator also stays BLANK even with a positive denominator. No alternate result is supplied.
			// Unit: Ratio stored as a fraction; displayed as a percentage with one decimal place.
			// Caveat: Zero or BLANK denominator returns BLANK. Disjoint dates: daily lifecycle denominator spans selected min/max while exit rows retain selected dates. Prefer contiguous equivalent intervals.
			DIVIDE([Employee Exits],[Average Daily Headcount])
		formatString: 0.0%
		displayFolder: 02 Retention
		lineageTag: 4673aed6-8927-4d9b-b720-0cb9331f97b1

	/// Employees already employed before the interval who remain employed at its end. Calculation: Hire Date strictly before Period Start Date and Exit Date blank or strictly after capped Period End Date. Purpose: Excludes in-period new hires so retention follows the opening workforce cohort. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. Empty/zero behavior: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written. Limitations: Future-only windows return BLANK; empty COUNTROWS can remain BLANK. Interpretation: This is the retained opening population, not all employees at closing.
	measure 'Opening Cohort Survivors' =
			// Employees already employed before the interval who remain employed at its end.
			// Why: Excludes in-period new hires so retention follows the opening workforce cohort.
			// Logic: Hire Date strictly before Period Start Date and Exit Date blank or strictly after capped Period End Date.
			// Context: Calendar min/max define explicit Employee Hire/Exit lifecycle windows, capped at global payroll month end. Dim_Date does not directly filter Employee. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications.
			// Empty/zero: COUNTROWS can return BLANK when no rows qualify; this is intentionally preserved. Explicit BLANK guards and suppressed totals are retained exactly as written.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: Future-only windows return BLANK; empty COUNTROWS can remain BLANK.
			VAR _s=[Period Start Date] VAR _e=[Period End Date] RETURN IF(_s>_e,BLANK(),COUNTROWS(FILTER('Dim_Employee','Dim_Employee'[Hire Date]<_s && (ISBLANK('Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]) || 'Dim_Employee'[Exit Date]>_e))))
		formatString: #,##0
		displayFolder: 02 Retention
		lineageTag: 75952b12-61dc-4eaa-a634-553bacbf180d

	/// Share of the opening workforce still employed at interval end. Calculation: Opening Cohort Survivors / Opening Headcount. Purpose: Cohort survival measures retention directly; it is not one minus period attrition. Unit: Ratio stored as a fraction; displayed as a percentage with one decimal place. Date/filter context: Opening cohort is employed before selected start; survivors remain employed after capped selected end. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. No active payroll-period relationship selects this cohort. Empty/zero behavior: DIVIDE returns BLANK for a zero or BLANK denominator; a BLANK numerator also stays BLANK even with a positive denominator. No alternate result is supplied. Limitations: Zero or BLANK opening headcount returns BLANK. Same extract organizational classifications apply to numerator and denominator. Interpretation: Higher means more of the opening population stayed; new hires cannot inflate it.
	measure 'Retention Rate' =
			// Share of the opening workforce still employed at interval end.
			// Why: Cohort survival measures retention directly; it is not one minus period attrition.
			// Logic: Opening Cohort Survivors / Opening Headcount.
			// Context: Opening cohort is employed before selected start; survivors remain employed after capped selected end. Department and employee filters apply using extract classifications. No active payroll-period relationship selects this cohort.
			// Empty/zero: DIVIDE returns BLANK for a zero or BLANK denominator; a BLANK numerator also stays BLANK even with a positive denominator. No alternate result is supplied.
			// Unit: Ratio stored as a fraction; displayed as a percentage with one decimal place.
			// Caveat: Zero or BLANK opening headcount returns BLANK. Same extract organizational classifications apply to numerator and denominator.
			DIVIDE([Opening Cohort Survivors],[Opening Headcount])
		formatString: 0.0%
		displayFolder: 02 Retention
		lineageTag: e51b5636-6a30-4f68-a024-4e01969dcb25

	/// Exit events classified voluntary in the source. Calculation: Employee Exits with KEEPFILTERS on Fact_Exit[Exit Type] = Voluntary. Purpose: Separates departure categories for targeted investigation. Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer. Date/filter context: Exit Date actively filters exit rows; Department and employee filters also apply. A future-only selected interval is suppressed using the global payroll cutoff; no payroll-period relationship filters the numerator. Empty/zero behavior: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added. Limitations: KEEPFILTERS intersects an existing exit-type filter. Other categories such as retirement are excluded from this measure. Interpretation: Read alongside all exits; categories need not exhaust every exit type.
	measure 'Voluntary Exits' =
			// Exit events classified voluntary in the source.
			// Why: Separates departure categories for targeted investigation.
			// Logic: Employee Exits with KEEPFILTERS on Fact_Exit[Exit Type] = Voluntary.
			// Context: Exit Date actively filters exit rows; Department and employee filters also apply. A future-only selected interval is suppressed using the global payroll cutoff; no payroll-period relationship filters the numerator.
			// Empty/zero: Existing DAX scalar and arithmetic BLANK behavior is preserved; no new empty-result substitution is added.
			// Unit: Count of the population described; displayed as an integer.
			// Caveat: KEEPFILTERS intersects an existing exit-type filter. Other categories such as retirement are excluded from this measure.
			CALCULATE([Employee Exits],KEEPFILTERS('Fact_Exit'[Exit Type]="Voluntary"))
